{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"research_plan (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "spines": [
  {
   "spine": "Public equity — earnings quality, competitive position, valuation, catalysts, downside risk",
   "must_answer": "Is the market mispricing Apple's future cash flow or its risk? Frame the call on FY2025/FY2026 revenue growth, services mix, gross margin, FCF conversion, ROIC versus WACC, the multiple versus peers and its own history, the catalyst calendar (iPhone cycle, Apple Intelligence, capital return) and the bear-case drawdown."
  },
  {
   "spine": "Policy / geopolitics-sensitive — national strategy, export controls, tariffs, platform regulation, market access",
   "must_answer": "Does policy create a moat or a hidden constraint? Quantify US tariffs and Section 232 exposure, US-China export controls and China market access, the EU Digital Markets Act and US antitrust remedies against App Store economics, and the India manufacturing shift, each with a dated event and a dollar or margin impact."
  },
  {
   "spine": "Consumer / brand — demand durability versus promotion-driven demand",
   "must_answer": "Is iPhone and services demand durable? Test replacement-cycle length, installed-base growth and switching costs, services attach and pricing power, Greater China demand and local competition (Huawei), and whether current growth is unit-driven or price/mix-driven."
  }
 ],
 "industry_map": "Consumer electronics plus digital services. Value chain: in-house silicon and OS design (Apple) → foundry and memory (TSMC and others) → components and displays → contract assembly (Foxconn, Pegatron, Luxshare; China with an India/Vietnam shift) → distribution (carriers, Apple retail, e-commerce) → an installed base of active devices → platform (iOS/macOS/watchOS) → services (App Store, iCloud, advertising, subscriptions, AppleCare, payments). Decisive bottleneck: control of the OS and installed base that converts hardware gross margin into recurring, higher-margin services revenue; that bottleneck is now squeezed by platform regulation (EU DMA, US App Store remedies) and by the AI capability race that determines the next upgrade cycle. Apple sits as the orchestrator: design and platform in-house, manufacturing outsourced, distribution partly owned, services attached to its own base.",
 "geo_lens": "Material. Map five reporting regions — Americas, Europe, Greater China, Japan, Rest of Asia Pacific — for revenue, margin and growth; map the manufacturing footprint (China, India, Vietnam) and the supplier base (Taiwan, South Korea, Japan, US); map capital markets (Nasdaq listing, USD reporting) and the comparable set (US mega-cap technology plus global handset makers). Greater China is the swing geography for both demand and supply-chain risk.",
 "policy_lens": "Material. Jurisdictions to map: United States (tariffs and Section 232 semiconductor/consumer-electronics actions, DOJ antitrust case against Apple, the Google default-search payment, export controls on advanced chips); European Union (Digital Markets Act and App Store/interoperability obligations, DMA fines); China (market access, local-content and security rules, Huawei competition, rare-earth and export-control leverage); India (production-linked incentives and manufacturing shift); United Kingdom (CMA mobile-market investigation). Policy hits both revenue (App Store take rate, default payments) and cost (tariffs, supply-chain relocation).",
 "regulatory_databases": [
  "SEC EDGAR — Apple 10-K, 10-Q, 8-K, DEF 14A and XBRL company facts",
  "Apple Investor Relations — earnings releases, prepared remarks, guidance and shareholder returns",
  "USPTO Patent Center and PatentsView — Apple patent filings",
  "European Commission competition/DMA case register and press releases",
  "US DOJ Antitrust Division and PACER dockets — United States v. Apple",
  "USTR and Federal Register — tariff and Section 232 actions",
  "UK Competition and Markets Authority — mobile ecosystems market study",
  "China SAMR/MIIT and India DPIIT/MeitY — market-access and manufacturing-incentive records"
 ],
 "gates": [
  "Confirm company identity, legal entity, geography, stage, and business model.",
  "Choose 1-3 report spines from report_strategy_playbook.md."
 ],
 "hypothesis": "Apple is a high-quality cash compounder with a durable installed-base and services moat, but at a premium multiple and mid-single-digit revenue growth it is likely fairly to fully valued; the working view is watchlist as a quality name, actionable long only on a valuation reset or a credible AI-led upgrade cycle that lifts growth above the current trajectory.",
 "author": "Lead Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-31T04:55:45+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

